            {
              "id": "2c09b9db-86bc-3cf1-8716-812c4cfb6e32",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a memory copy operation where the source and destination may overlap, which can result in undefined behavior according to the C standard. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions due to data flow between them. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and both the source and destination are confirmed to be non-null, so the main risk is the potential for undefined behavior if overlap occurs. The source is a general identifier, not a string literal or constant, which increases the likelihood of overlap. This type of issue can lead to subtle bugs or security vulnerabilities, especially if the memory regions partially overlap.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping memory safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```c\nmemmove(&tmpGraph, graph, sizeof(struct ncclTopoGraph));\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination memory regions overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
